From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Junod <donujp@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 13:52:36 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] fix(peer): bound the responses queued for a single peer

When a peer sends a request, the piece or metadata response is pushed
onto unbounded channels (the upload rate-limit scheduler, then the
peer's writer) with no cap on how many can be outstanding. A peer that
sends requests faster than its socket drains, or never reads at all,
makes us queue responses (each a chunk to read from disk and write)
without limit.

Give every peer connection a PeerResponseBudget: a semaphore whose
permits travel with each queued piece or metadata response and are
released only once the writer has flushed it to the socket (or the
response is dropped). Admission uses try_acquire: a peer that has more
responses pending than the budget is disconnected rather than blocking
the reader task behind the torrent-wide upload limiter.

The budget is the per-peer request window, i.e. max_request_window (from
"feat(peer): make the per-peer request window configurable"; default
128, unchanged), and it is advertised to the peer as `reqq` in our
extended handshake so well-behaved clients stay inside it. rqbit did not
advertise reqq before.

Note that this ties the number of requests we accept from a peer to the
number we are willing to have outstanding towards a peer; setting
max_request_window very low (for seek responsiveness) therefore also
lowers it.

The tests cover the production admission path across the scheduler and
writer queues, release on completion, and that the writer holds the
permit until the socket write finishes (and releases it if the write is
cancelled).
